WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.131 --> 00:00:14.865
OLIVIA: Hello, Little Fox readers, and welcome to another edition of People in the News . Today Ben

2
00:00:14.866 --> 00:00:23.612
will be talking about one of the longest reigning monarchs in British history: Her Majesty Queen Elizabeth II. Elizabeth

3
00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:29.275
has been Queen of the Commonwealth of Nations since 1952. BEN: When Princess Elizabeth was born in London,

4
00:00:29.276 --> 00:00:38.118
England, on April 21, 1926, nobody thought that she would one day be queen. Her father, Prince Albert,

5
00:00:38.119 --> 00:00:45.190
was the second son of King George V, and therefore not likely to become king. However, things changed when

6
00:00:45.191 --> 00:00:55.288
Elizabeth was ten years old and Prince Albert's older brother—King Edward VIII—abdicated the throne to marry a woman

7
00:00:55.289 --> 00:01:01.254
who was divorced. Prince Albert suddenly became King George VI, and Elizabeth was now heir to the throne.

8
00:01:01.255 --> 00:01:09.254
As queen, Elizabeth would need to be knowledgeable in many different subjects, so her education became a high priority

9
00:01:09.255 --> 00:01:16.325
for the royal family. Under the instruction of private tutors, she began studying constitutional law and history as

10
00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:25.248
well as religion. She also studied music and French. Knowing French would be important because French is the

11
00:01:25.249 --> 00:01:29.395
official language in some of the countries of the Commonwealth. Elizabeth took on more responsibilities as she got older.

12
00:01:29.396 --> 00:01:35.473
At the age of 14, she delivered her first radio broadcast—for the children of the Commonwealth. World War

13
00:01:35.474 --> 00:01:42.980
II was raging, and many children had been evacuated from their homes and were separated from their families.

14
00:01:42.981 --> 00:01:49.210
Elizabeth's broadcast was intended to provide comfort by assuring them that everything would be okay. A few years later,

15
00:01:49.211 --> 00:01:56.947
as the war continued, Elizabeth spent time volunteering, and she received training as a driver and mechanic. By

16
00:01:56.948 --> 00:02:05.116
1952 Elizabeth's father had become very ill and was no longer able to perform all of his responsibilities

17
00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:14.125
as king. Elizabeth had already been accompanying her parents to many meetings within England, but now she visited New

18
00:02:14.126 --> 00:02:23.047
Zealand and Australia in her father's place. In 1953, while Elizabeth was staying in Kenya with her husband,

19
00:02:23.048 --> 00:02:30.277
whom she had married in 1947, King George VI died. Elizabeth returned home right away—not only was she

20
00:02:30.278 --> 00:02:36.171
mourning the loss of her father, but she was now the Queen! Her coronation took place on June 2,

21
00:02:36.172 --> 00:02:41.679
1953, in Westminster Abbey. OLIVIA: So did this mean that Elizabeth was now in charge of the entire

22
00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.597
Commonwealth? BEN: Good question, Olivia. Kings and queens aren't as powerful as they used to be. The nations

23
00:02:46.598 --> 00:02:52.165
within the Commonwealth all have elected governments that write laws and make decisions. Elizabeth's authority is actually very limited;

24
00:02:52.166 --> 00:02:57.813
she's just a symbolic leader. Still, Elizabeth has a tremendous amount of responsibility. She presides over the opening

25
00:02:57.814 --> 00:03:02.081
ceremony for each session of the British Parliament. And while Parliament's members are the ones who write new

26
00:03:02.082 --> 00:03:06.904
laws, no law is official until Elizabeth signs it. She reviews everything that was discussed in Parliament each day,

27
00:03:06.905 --> 00:03:13.647
and once a week, she meets with the British prime minister. In order to be informed of all

28
00:03:13.648 --> 00:03:20.393
that's going on in her "realms," Elizabeth reads the newspapers every morning. Then she goes through the hundreds

29
00:03:20.394 --> 00:03:27.139
of letters she receives each day from people around the world. Elizabeth chooses a certain number of these

30
00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:32.209
to read and answer because she can't possibly read them all. She also has numerous meetings each day with

31
00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:39.127
foreign dignitaries, leaders, and other important people. As Queen, Elizabeth is the head of several hundred organizations and

32
00:03:39.128 --> 00:03:45.127
charities, so she is always attending ceremonies, making speeches, and presenting awards. In her free time, Elizabeth loves

33
00:03:45.128 --> 00:03:55.042
to be outdoors, walking the hills around Balmoral, her estate in Scotland. She is also a passionate racehorse breeder

34
00:03:55.043 --> 00:04:01.858
and attends many horse races. OLIVIA: Elizabeth has done a lot to modernize the royal family, hasn't she?

35
00:04:01.859 --> 00:04:06.602
BEN: That's right, Olivia. During her reign Elizabeth has worked hard to make sure the monarchy is still

36
00:04:06.603 --> 00:04:11.368
relevant in modern-day society. For instance, she decided to have her coronation televised for the first time in history.

37
00:04:11.369 --> 00:04:19.425
She even allowed filmmakers into Buckingham Palace, her London residence, so they could make a television movie about

38
00:04:19.426 --> 00:04:25.081
her life behind the scenes. She has also done away with many of the formalities associated with royalty.

39
00:04:25.082 --> 00:04:33.344
It used to be that one had to bow or curtsy when being presented to the Queen, but now

40
00:04:33.345 --> 00:04:38.255
people are allowed to speak to her in a more casual manner and simply shake hands. Elizabeth enjoys

41
00:04:38.256 --> 00:04:48.326
meeting people from all different social classes, not just rich people and world leaders. Sometimes she has a

42
00:04:48.327 --> 00:04:54.907
"walkabout," where she goes out and walks among a crowd, greeting people that she might not otherwise mingle with.

43
00:04:54.908 --> 00:05:02.312
She hosts quite a few parties and makes sure ordinary members of the public are invited to many

44
00:05:02.313 --> 00:05:08.722
of these events. Despite being extremely wealthy, Elizabeth has always been very frugal. She was married when Great

45
00:05:08.723 --> 00:05:13.522
Britain was still recovering from World War II, so her wedding wasn't very extravagant. And she is said to

46
00:05:13.523 --> 00:05:19.949
have saved clothing coupons to pay for her dress, just as most other women had to do. Even

47
00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:26.875
today Elizabeth often reminds people to turn off the lights when they leave a room, and she insists

48
00:05:26.876 --> 00:05:38.269
that palace guards don't stand in the middle of rugs because she doesn't want the rugs to wear out

49
00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:45.269
too quickly. In 2012 Queen Elizabeth II celebrated her Diamond Jubilee, marking sixty years on the throne. Only

50
00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:50.355
one other British monarch—Queen Victoria—has reigned longer than that. From World War II to space travel to the

51
00:05:50.356 --> 00:05:57.854
rise of the internet, the world has changed quite a bit since Elizabeth's reign began. OLIVIA: Wow! Thanks, Ben.

52
00:05:57.855 --> 00:06:09.449
It's amazing to think just how long Elizabeth has been queen! See you next week, Little Fox readers!

